        "abstract": "<italic xmlns:mml=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" xmlns:xlink=\"http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink\">Doxa</i>: from the Greek, opinion, way of thinking. According to Parmenides, the world of <italic xmlns:mml=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" xmlns:xlink=\"http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink\">doxa</i> was the world of the opinions of mortals. When substituted by “what is” for what seems to be, or when mortals concur to manipulate things, or understand themselves, it becomes a falsehood or an error. Husserl held that all expressions are a doxic act in their full sense, i.e, a certainty, a belief.<xref ref-type=\"fn\" rid=\"fn1\" xmlns:mml=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" xmlns:xlink=\"http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink\"><sup>1</sup></xref> Paul Foulquie, Diccionario del lenguaje filosófico (Barcelona: Editorial Labor, 1962), 42.",
